<Project>

  <!--
    Derived versioning for the packages this repo publishes. Replaces the <ServiceVersion>1.0.0</ServiceVersion>
    literal that CommonPackageHandling.targets used to turn into <Version>; the version now comes from
    version.json plus the number of commits since that file last changed.

    WHY A SEPARATE FILE, imported from CommonPackageHandling.targets, rather than the two obvious places.
    Directory.Build.props and ServiceVersion.props BOTH reach MakeRelease, which is the one project in
    this repo with central package management OFF and which imports ServiceVersion.props directly
    (MakeRelease/MakeRelease.csproj:2). A version-less PackageReference under CPM-off fails restore with
    NU1015, and one carrying Version= under CPM-on fails NU1008 - so a single declaration cannot serve
    both regimes. CommonPackageHandling.targets is imported by exactly the 12 projects that pack, and by
    nothing else, MakeRelease included.

    IT ALSO SURVIVES `lz gen`. Every generated .csproj is rewritten with overwrite:true, so a
    PackageReference added to one is lost on the next generation. Lz.Gen writes only inside a project
    folder plus the .sln; it never touches a solution-root file.

    THE IMPORT OF THIS FILE IS UNCONDITIONAL, deliberately. The ServiceVersion.props import it replaced
    carried Condition="!Exists('ClientSDK')", a copy of a project-template guard that is meaningless in a
    consumer repo: it resolves against this directory, is true only because the folder here is plural,
    and anything named ClientSDK created at the repo root would silently disable it. Because the SDK's
    own default version is also 1.0.0, that regression would be invisible.

    GitVersionBaseDirectory pins the height to the repo root so all 12 compute ONE version. Not
    per-project pathFilters: pack asks each referenced project for its own PackageVersion, so a commit
    touching one project would move another's nuspec dependency while leaving its own version alone, and
    one version would then name two different packages.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GitVersionBaseDirectory>$(SolutionDir)</GitVersionBaseDirectory>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Nerdbank.GitVersioning" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A SHALLOW clone is loud - NBGV throws MSB4018. NO git repository at all is not: a tree with
    version.json and no .git builds successfully, with no warning, at height 0 - a prerelease that sorts
    BELOW the released version. Refuse it rather than ship it.

    This is why .dockerignore no longer excludes .git/. Containers/AppHost/Dockerfile does COPY . ., so
    without history in the build context every in-image build would hit this guard. Copying .git costs
    about 5 MB of build context, against a DockerPackages folder that is already several times that, and
    it buys a real version on the assemblies the image carries. The .git directory reaches only the build
    stage; the final image copies /app/publish alone.
  -->
  <Target Name="RefuseAVersionWithNoGitHistory"
          BeforeTargets="GetBuildVersion;Build"
          Condition="!Exists('$(GitVersionBaseDirectory).git')">
    <Error Text="No git repository at $(GitVersionBaseDirectory), so the version cannot be derived. Nerdbank.GitVersioning does not fail here - it would quietly produce a height-0 prerelease that sorts BELOW the released version, and every package this build produced would carry it. Build from a full clone." />
  </Target>

</Project>
